# Database Architecture Rules

## Overview

MercFlow uses PostgreSQL as its database, accessed via Medusa v2's built-in data layer. Medusa v2 uses **MikroORM v6** under the hood, but custom modules interact with the database exclusively through Medusa's **Data Model Language (DML)** and **service factory** — never through raw MikroORM or SQL unless inside a migration file.

---

## Data Modeling — The DML

### Always use DML for data models

Medusa's DML (`model.define` from `@medusajs/framework/utils`) is the only way to define data models in MercFlow modules. It maps directly to PostgreSQL tables and columns.

```ts
import { model } from "@medusajs/framework/utils"

export const ProductContent = model.define("product_content", {
  id: model.id().primaryKey(),
  product_id: model.text(),
  description_rich: model.json().nullable(),
  seo_title: model.text().nullable(),
})
```

### DML type reference

| DML method | PostgreSQL type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `model.id()` | `uuid` | Always use for primary keys |
| `model.text()` | `text` | Default string type — no length limit |
| `model.number()` | `numeric` | Use for integers and decimals |
| `model.float()` | `real` | Use for tax rates and percentages |
| `model.boolean()` | `boolean` | |
| `model.json()` | `jsonb` | Use for structured objects (TipTap, metadata) |
| `model.array()` | `text[]` | Array of text values |
| `model.dateTime()` | `timestamptz` | Always timezone-aware |
| `model.enum()` | `text` with check | Pass the enum object as parameter |

**Modifiers:**
- `.nullable()` — allows NULL, maps to `| null` in TypeScript
- `.default(value)` — sets a default value
- `.primaryKey()` — marks the field as primary key
- `.unique()` — adds a unique constraint
- `.index()` — adds a database index

### Auto-generated fields

The DML automatically adds these to every model — never define them manually:

```ts
created_at   // timestamptz, set on insert
updated_at   // timestamptz, updated on every change
deleted_at   // timestamptz, null unless soft-deleted
```

### Relationships

```ts
// One-to-many: a Brand has many Products
export const Brand = model.define("brand", {
  id: model.id().primaryKey(),
  name: model.text(),
  products: model.hasMany(() => Product, { mappedBy: "brand" }),
})

export const Product = model.define("product", {
  id: model.id().primaryKey(),
  brand: model.belongsTo(() => Brand, { mappedBy: "products" }),
})

// One-to-one: a Product has one ProductContent
export const ProductContent = model.define("product_content", {
  id: model.id().primaryKey(),
  product: model.belongsTo(() => Product, { mappedBy: "content" }),
})
```

